 8)\f1\tab B\f0 oth the 1976\f1  [Version 8P]\f0  and 1989 \f1 [Version 8Z] \f0 release\f1 s\f0  of \f1\tab\f0 P?S/8\f1  \f0 date support\f1  \f0 an algorithm that\f1  provides for an \f0 1\f1 1 \f0 year \f1\tab\f0 range of dates passed in memory as an internal\f1  \f0 parameter of the \f1\tab\f0 system kernel.  System dates are ordinarily unique to within\f1\tab\f0 any date within an eight-year period\f1 ; b\f0 y providing the anomaly \f1\tab\f0 of this method,\f1  \f0 adjustment of the base year [originally set to \f1\tab\f0 1960] could be deferred by nearly\f1  \f0 four years after the system \f1\tab\f0 changes \f1 could first\f0  be made.\page\cf3\f2 Footnotes [continued].\par
\cf0\f0\par
\f1\tab\f0 The \f1 i\f0 mplementation of th\f1 is\f0  method was at an early stage of \f1\tab\f0 development when the\f1  \f0 1976 version of P?S/8\f1  [Version 8P]\f0  was \f1\tab created\f0 .  As such, the DATE command prevents setting\f1\tab\f0 any date \f1\tab\f0 outside of the range of 1976 through 1986.\par
\f1\par
\tab\f0 By the release\f1  of P?S/8 Version 8Y\f0 , all system programs that \f1\tab\f0 conform to the date\f1  \f0 mechanism define internal storage locations \f1\tab\f0 allowing adjustment of the base date\f1 ; this \f0 allow\f1 s manual patching \tab of any date range from 1960 through\f0  2087.\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 Future releases of P?S/8 will abandon this method entirely.  The \f1\tab\f0 new base date\f1  \f0 is 01-Jan-1900 and the entire date is available in \f1\tab\f0 the system kernel in an\f1  1\f0 8-bit form\f1 at\f0 .  As such, all programs \f1\tab\f0 will be modifed to obtain the date directly\f1  \f0 without restriction.\par
\f1\par
\tab\f0 By enlarging the supported date range to 1900 through 2411, all \f1\tab\f0 relevant file\f1  \f0 dates from the twentieth century well into the \f1\tab\f0 future [as of this writing] are\f1  \f0 supported.\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 While basic P?S/8 TFS files do not support file dates, the P?S/8\f1\tab\f0 SHELL directory will fully support the expanded date support.  As \f1\tab\f0 such, P?S/8\f1  \f0 date support will be more useful than OS/8, unix and \f1\tab\f0 other systems, especially\f1  \f0 for existing computer files from the \f1\tab\f0 1960s and the 1970s [which cannot be\f1  \f0 supported by most of the \f1\tab\f0 "modern" operating systems in use today].\par
