\f1\tab\f0 UCNVR8 is a program written by Mark J. Hyde of Syracuse\f1  \tab\f0 University to convert\f1  \f0 files on DECtapes between various systems \f1\tab\f0 including P?S/8, OS/8, ADSS-9/15 and\f1  \f0 possibly the Disk Monitor \f1\tab\f0 System on DECtape.  It runs in 4K memory as a\f1  \f0 stand-alone utility \f1\tab\f0 and only supports the TC01/TC08/TU55/TU56 DECtape.  [If run\f1  \f0 on \f1\tab\f0 the PDP-12, the TC12F option is used to access DECtapes; LAP6-\f1\tab\f0 DIAL LINCtapes\f1  \f0 are also supported using the DIAL default edit \f1\tab\f0 buffer only.]\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 UCNVR8 uses a\f1  \f0 unique form of DECtape support where the motion bit \f1\tab\f0 can be maintained; this is\f1  \f0 useful when accessing PDP-9/15\f1  \tab\f0 DECtapes where the next block to be read can be\f1  \f0 accesssed without \f1\tab\f0 tape turnaround.\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 UCNVR8 is a stand-alone utility and need not be a P?S/8 system \f1\tab\f0 program.  It can\f1  \f0 be made more generably available for use on \f1\tab\f0 newer releases of P?S/8 or OS/8\f1  [or LAP6-DIAL/DIAL-MS]\f0 .\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 P?S/8 TFS text files created by UCNVR8 are deficient \'12n that the \f1\tab\f0 internal line\f1  \f0 numbers and text pointers are not created; however, \f1\tab\f0 they are properly accounted\f1  \f0 for thus each file is properly \f1\tab\f0 allocated without overload.\page\cf3\f2 Footnotes [continued].\par
\cf0\f0\par
\f1\tab\f0 The NUM program was \f1 also \f0 written by Mark\f1  Hyde\f0  as a partial remedy \f1\tab\f0 to this problem.\f1   \f0 Files are passed to NUM to be read\f1  in and\f0  \f1\tab\f0 corrected to proper TFS standards then\f1  \f0 written \f1 back to the same \tab storage blocks\f0 .\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 Note: \f1 The \f0 NUM\f1  program\f0  is ignorant of P?S/8 output fil\f1 e \tab\f0 conventions; the\f1  \f0 first file in the passed parameter list is used \f1\tab\f0 without regard for input or\f1  \f0 output status.  To within this \f1\tab\f0 proviso, NUM can be run on any P?S/8 release.\par
\par
\f1\tab\f0 Charles Lasner pointed out the NUM program is superfluous as the \f1\tab\f0 same results\f1  \f0 can be achieved through the use of a short BATCH \f1\tab\f0 program.  Assume the file\f1  \f0 FOOBAR must have line number \f1\tab\f0 entries created.  Create a control file FOO with\f1  \f0 the single line:\par
\par
\f1\tab  \f0 100 SEQUENCE\par
\par
\f1\tab Note: \f0 This assumes the 1976 release of P?S/8\f1  [Version 8P]\f0 ; for \f1\tab\f0 all newer releases\f1  \f0 use \f1 L\f0 NUMBER]\par
